<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Wales</h1><p class="st-meta">1216 CE – 1283 CE · capital Aber Garth Celyn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Welsh state built by the princes of Gwynedd and recognised by England in 1267, the high point of native Welsh political independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn ap Iorwerth</a>, Llywelyn the Great, made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Gwynedd</a> dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdyfi">Aberdyfi</a> in 1216 the other Welsh rulers accepted his overlordship. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III</a> recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> in 1267.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a feudal overlordship over lesser Welsh lords such as those of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/powys-fadog">Powys Fadog</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/powys-wenwynwyn">Powys Wenwynwyn</a>. Its princes ruled from Gwynedd's courts, including Aber Garth Celyn, and used Welsh law, stone castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolwyddelan-castle">Dolwyddelan</a> and a growing chancery.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Llywelyn's refusal to do homage to Edward I led to war in 1277, and a revolt begun by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dafydd-ap-gruffydd">Dafydd</a> in 1282 ended in conquest. He was killed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilmeri">Cilmeri</a> in 1282, and his brother Dafydd executed in 1283. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-rhuddlan">Statute of Rhuddlan</a> of 1284 annexed the principality to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-wales.jpg" alt="Principality of Wales" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: A455bcd9 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wales_after_the_Treaty_of_Montgomery_1267_Colorblind.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1216 CE – 1283 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aber Garth Celyn</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, Dafydd ap Llywelyn, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tywysogaeth Cymru</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=principality-of-wales" aria-label="Principality of Wales on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4599 372 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Wales lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4919 628L4912 613M4919 628L4929 609M4919 628L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4912 613h0M4929 609h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4919 628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4919 628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=principality-of-wales">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
